#[chapter/12/lore]{
  notes: [
    "Boof's wish, sworn true under the Blueberry Oath: one warm furrow kept open, always, for whatever arrives next without a receipt."
    "Honest price of a forever-small wish: everything the Genie had left, plus one Thursday. He retires into the grove as its next echo — a granting you can visit."
    "BaneWAP splits himself as apology; BoonWAP receives him warm; BoneWAP posts both receipts. Both can post because the wish aligned them."
    "Seam-otters swim exactly on the confluence seam, one paw in each water; their fur dries two colors, so boatmen swear there are two kinds. Ink-herons stand so still in the berry-dark shall..."
    "The ferry-bell is rung by anyone who needs to cross the seam without wetting both springs, or who doubts the bridge will hold them. Load-bells under the deck rang light under the re-sweet..."
    "The rail-pins hold the bridge's small news; BoneWAP posted both receipts there after the market stone's oath-scale weighed the two slices equal, and oath-moths settled one to each."
    "Every clear dusk the ferrymen chalk <river ~ moon> on the landing slate — not to make the moonrun, which the river makes itself, but so no stranger mistakes a passage for a place to stop...."
    "Two springs, one bed: what the boatmen call the rivers. The ferryman keeps two bailing-cans, one stained berry-dark and one chalk-white, and has never bailed one water with the other’s can."
    "Pricing the furrow, the Genie murmured his oldest advice, the one thing he ever priced at nothing — build where the land already leans; the ants had kept that field warm every winter Boof..."
  ]
}

